  {
   "id": "sticky0",
   "name": "Sticky — 1",
   "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
   "typeVersion": 1,
   "position": [
    -400,
    -260
   ],
   "parameters": {
    "content": "## Two layers, and they answer DIFFERENT questions\nCloudflare Access Service Auth (in front, `/webhook/owner-*`) answers **which machine**. The HMAC here answers **which request**. Both are required: if the service token ever leaks, the signature is the only thing left standing.\n\nRaw body ON — the signature is over the exact bytes the dashboard sent.",
    "width": 520,
    "height": 220
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "sticky1",
   "name": "Sticky — 2",
   "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
   "typeVersion": 1,
   "position": [
    480,
    -420
   ],
   "parameters": {
    "content": "## The allow-list is the security model\nExactly ONE verb: `release_handoff`. `prompt-injection.md` — there is no other write action, so there is nothing for a compromise to escalate into. This is also why CRT #11 is auditable: the surface is one verb.\n\nMirrors `OWNER_ACTIONS` in `lib/ownerAction.ts`. Two places, on purpose.",
    "width": 520,
    "height": 220
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "sticky2",
   "name": "Sticky — 3",
   "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
   "typeVersion": 1,
   "position": [
    1360,
    -560
   ],
   "parameters": {
    "content": "## A signature does not say WHICH conversation\nThree conditions, all required: the record exists · `stage === 'handoff'` · touched within 30 days. Otherwise 404 (missing) / 409 (not locked).\n\nWithout this, one valid signature unlocks ARBITRARY conversations — the same hole the endpoint exists to close, just moved inside the fence.",
    "width": 520,
    "height": 220
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "sticky3",
   "name": "Sticky — 4",
   "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
   "typeVersion": 1,
   "position": [
    2240,
    -700
   ],
   "parameters": {
    "content": "## Replay: the honest bound\nThe dedupe row is written at the END. A replay arriving while the first is STILL IN FLIGHT finds nothing and proceeds.\n\nWhat makes that safe is that the write is IDEMPOTENT — `stage='new'` twice equals once. There is no calendar-id backstop here like the booking path has. Do NOT read this as \"deduplicated\".",
    "width": 520,
    "height": 240
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "sticky4",
   "name": "Sticky — 5",
   "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
   "typeVersion": 1,
   "position": [
    2900,
    -760
   ],
   "parameters": {
    "content": "## Write-then-verify\nAirtable and the engine share no transaction. A 200 is not a confirmed write — `booking-integrity.md` requires the re-read on every mutation path.\n\n`stage` AND `last_intent` TOGETHER: two escalation ladders read `last_intent`, so clearing only `stage` leaves the conversation half-armed and it re-locks on the next uncertain turn.",
    "width": 560,
    "height": 240
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "sticky5",
   "name": "Sticky — 6",
   "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
   "typeVersion": 1,
   "position": [
    3120,
    60
   ],
   "parameters": {
    "content": "## Failure is VISIBLE\nA release that did not happen must never look like one that did. Separate alert class, separate branch, 502 to the caller.\n\n⚠ This leaf is TERMINAL — no alert about a failed alert (infinite loop).",
    "width": 520,
    "height": 200
   }
  }
 ],
